# Task narrative -- record what we did and why

Every completed task needs a **reasoning-rich narrative**: task, goal, why it mattered, what changed, how it works now, and especially **why this approach** (tradeoffs / rejected alternatives).

Where that narrative lives depends on whether the work ships through a pull request.

| Situation | Home |
|---|---|
| Work ships in a PR (default for code, config, CI, rules) | **PR body** -- `.github/pull_request_template.md` |
| No PR (status-only direct push, ops diagnosis, audit conclusion) | `knowledge/task-log/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` |

CHANGELOG = short "what the code does now."
PROBLEM_LOG = bug diagnosis after a **fix**.
GitHub Issues labeled `deferred` = known bugs and parked features that are **not** done yet.
**This rule = full job narrative + reasoning.** All four can apply to one job.

## When (mandatory)

At the **end of every completed task** — parent session or specialist — that:

- Changed product code, config, CI, Docker, or security posture
- Closed or materially advanced a roadmap/security finding
- Produced an audit/ops conclusion future agents must not re-derive from scratch

Also required when the **parent** closes a multi-domain task done in-session (one narrative is enough — see `specialist-routing.mdc` zero-hop default).

## When to skip

- Pure typo / formatting-only
- Aborted work with nothing learned
- Pure crack-index / “who owns X” with no decision
- Status-only poll (e.g. IBKR connected check) with no remediation

If skipping, Lifecycle must say `task_log=skipped` or `task_log=n/a` with a one-word reason in the report body when useful.

## How -- PR path (default)

1. Fill every section of `.github/pull_request_template.md`. **Why this approach** and **Verified by** are never optional.
2. Use `Closes #NNN` only when the full issue is complete. Use `Refs #NNN` for partial work and leave that issue open.
3. Cross-link from CHANGELOG / PROBLEM_LOG `Related:` when those files also get an entry.
4. Lifecycle footer includes `task_log=<PR URL>`.

Do **not** write both a PR body and a `knowledge/task-log/` file for the same job. Two homes for one story is how they drift.

Existing `knowledge/task-log/` entries stay where they are. They are append-only history -- never delete or thin one out to "deduplicate" against a PR body.

## How -- file path (no PR)

1. Prefer scaffolder: `py -3 tools/task_log_new.py --slug <kebab> --title "<title>"`
2. Or copy `knowledge/task-log/_template.md` → `knowledge/task-log/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`
3. Fill **every** section — **Why this approach** is never optional for completed remediations
4. Prepend a row to `knowledge/task-log/INDEX.md` (newest first)
5. Lifecycle footer includes `task_log=<relative-path-from-repo-root>`

Example Lifecycle:

```text
**Lifecycle:** memory=changed | promotion=none | dashboard=clean | handoff=none | task_log=https://github.com/aaltaay/Nova/pull/49 | problem_log=2026-07-18 -- SEC-001-008 | deferred_log=none
```

## Ownership

| Role | Duty |
|------|------|
| Parent / implementer | Write the narrative for work they shipped, including one aggregate narrative for multi-domain work done in-session |
| Specialists (opt-in) | Write when their run is the source of truth (audit conclusion, ops diagnosis); otherwise hand facts to the parent |
| Docs | May tidy prose; must not invent technical reasons |

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ “Fixed it” with no why / tradeoffs
- ❌ An empty or template-only PR body ("see commits")
- ❌ Duplicating the same narrative in a PR body *and* a task-log file
- ❌ Secrets, tokens, or full `.env` contents in a narrative
- ❌ Duplicating a 40-line stack trace (summarize)
- ❌ Skipping the narrative because CHANGELOG already has a bullet
- ❌ Editing old entries to rewrite history — add a new dated note instead

